#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161869 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162069 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "129940fb-a409-55bc-9852-f68f4f9ffd11" :knowledge-point/title "Joint PDFs for Continuous Random Variables: Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/id #uuid "7f92bdfc-9d59-585c-8917-9198c4a053f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-9183" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n![](images/8b/8b534b43871bd40b698483a627d2d9564c153311c916f445f4c071f90bcee487.svg)\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\,\\textrm{d}y= 1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D = \\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\lt y \\}.$\n\n![](images/9c/9c823d67872095514c6392038ff43497512de656e28b26160465760df0faa4ea.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c > 0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y = \\iint_{D} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y = \\int_{0}^{\\infty}\\int_{x}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{c}{(1+x+y)^3} \\;\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x= 1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{x}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{x}^{\\infty }(1+x+y)^{-3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}(1+x+y)^{-2}]}_{y=x}^{y=\\infty }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{c}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{[(1+x+y)^{-2}]}_{y=x}^{y=\\infty }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{c}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }0-(1+2x)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{c}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-(1+2x)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(1+2x)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }(1+2x)^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{c}{2}{[-\\frac{1}{2}(1+2x)^{-1}]}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-\\frac{c}{4}{[\\frac{1}{1+2x}]}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-\\frac{c}{4}[0-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{4}=1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161870 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162070 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2bcf1eb2-cf04-5dd0-bb43-682c7e9cd953" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Using a Joint PDF"}] :question/id #uuid "068714ca-5127-509c-a4e1-51374c6afbed" :question/math-academy-id "e-9184" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n![](images/69/69f95d48c02ba9630b7aef5a49980581f6a719137bd35a101632e6157cbec17a.svg)\n\nFind $P(X > 1, Y < 1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A\\subset \\mathbb R^2,$ then\n\n$$\nP\\left((X,Y)\\in A\\right) = \\iint_A f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D = [0,3]\\times [0,2],$ and the event $A = \\left\\{ X > 1, Y < 1\\right\\}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/b1/b1fc6548c909b9887c708c4c5dcce5b715d112defc7ce1fc639784bbaf55e111.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>1,Y<1) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{∬}_{A}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\text{Area}(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (3-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161875 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a66952d-4e4b-5249-8f44-27d6fd198ea8" :knowledge-point/title "Joint PDFs for Continuous Random Variables: Rectangular Domains"}] :question/id #uuid "1ebb1eb5-e7f2-557c-aafa-e11dcb2b04e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9207" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n![](images/6e/6efcc38c3e389b9f858b71d089077924d9780b4b3daf771c4682e88098393977.svg)\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2} f(x,y) \\;\\textrm{d}x\\textrm{d}y= 1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D = \\left\\{(x,y) \\::\\:  0\\lt x\\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},0\\lt y \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right\\}.$\n\n![](images/cc/ccb7cc52f8404dcca5d555d4fd81e6ae03e9689ccdd7c462fccbc2dc611db235.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c > 0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y) \\geq 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}c\\sin (x+y) \\;\\textrm{d}y\\,\\textrm{d}x= 1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)]_{y=0}^{y=\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =c{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =c[(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))] \\\\  & =c[(1-0)-(0-1)] \\\\  & =c[1-(-1)] \\\\  & =2c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n2c=1 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad c=\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162299 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a66952d-4e4b-5249-8f44-27d6fd198ea8" :knowledge-point/title "Joint PDFs for Continuous Random Variables: Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0234212d-c651-55f6-a066-37e862d07375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244326} {:answer/id #uuid "77b086d2-27a3-5f81-89ba-bb099e3f4b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ and $g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245374} {:answer/id #uuid "81567dc6-0f86-5ac4-a019-858fd36bbb67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245460} {:answer/id #uuid "b5db7037-1e29-52fe-9521-3bee27ddca91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ and $h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245940} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e7b96d-2d39-5caf-b4ec-47ca0bae9ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5e7b96d-2d39-5caf-b4ec-47ca0bae9ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246347} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf1e7b28-8c8b-5a11-bf90-b10bdf13bea3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "948fa188-23c4-57d9-97ec-d87e8b50b8a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-110367" :question/problem "Let $D=[0,2]\\times [0,2].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\nI. $f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $g(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $h(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the second condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\text{ }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2y+2)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{y}^{2}+2y]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =8 \\\\  & \\ne 1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the first condition. For example,\n\n$$\ng(1,2)=-1\\cdot 2=-2≱0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $h(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}xy\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{4}xy\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\cdot y]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162302 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162070 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2bcf1eb2-cf04-5dd0-bb43-682c7e9cd953" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Using a Joint PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e9946ee-5abd-5d1e-8a63-aeb7b187b34d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245147} {:answer/id #uuid "95c9405f-2c54-5c4e-9531-62d47a0707ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245626} {:answer/id #uuid "a372f475-59b5-516e-9d65-67f0851879ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245771} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ce00e2-57b5-502b-a2c4-43968b29ef91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245776} {:answer/id #uuid "e7736a4f-9410-54c9-a441-993d7f8e82b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246362}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e9946ee-5abd-5d1e-8a63-aeb7b187b34d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245147} :answer-field/id #uuid "36cd1892-5d13-50c7-8f16-5f0f2acd35c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8186b99f-d61f-5c56-85da-9a33f2dcd9a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-110370" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4xy,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X>\\frac{1}{2},Y>\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,1]\\times [0,1],$ and the event $A=\\{X>0.5,Y>0.5\\}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/7e/7e81ce16404bc6c5c826f79a88519bd6216156f5bb0f8af1eae41f19ca495de5.png)\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>\\frac{1}{2},Y>\\frac{1}{2}) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}4xy\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}[{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}4xy\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{[2{x}^{2}y]}_{x=1/2}^{x=1}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{3y}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{4}]}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247173 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a66952d-4e4b-5249-8f44-27d6fd198ea8" :knowledge-point/title "Joint PDFs for Continuous Random Variables: Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1900b810-5283-5de2-8515-068de55ed74c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244536} {:answer/id #uuid "1fd1a474-87d3-51ac-b166-c39933bba5bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244596} {:answer/id #uuid "20fcd3e8-ce28-54b8-8247-e60be7ee2cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244604} {:answer/id #uuid "bd430d41-91d0-5075-8f65-2f92d15b2a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246003} {:answer/id #uuid "e2877088-9bd6-5205-b13a-839003b0bf01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd430d41-91d0-5075-8f65-2f92d15b2a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246003} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea53fa9d-9912-588b-8d36-17eab032f4b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80074266-7584-5c7b-8bab-8678f0fa0b55" :question/math-academy-id "q-110858" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c{e}^{-x-y},\\, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0\\}.$\n\n![](images/99/996bd092be07e2596e30739cc4249e8ed8ef859540067a94e0f9cdd05f9e1f84.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x-y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x-y}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[-c{e}^{-x-y}]_{y=0}^{y=\\infty }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }c{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & =[-c{e}^{-x}]_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a66952d-4e4b-5249-8f44-27d6fd198ea8" :knowledge-point/title "Joint PDFs for Continuous Random Variables: Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3587f4ec-5133-574c-85c2-b625e2022174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244789} {:answer/id #uuid "5bfbe54b-2a89-5a19-a15d-418da1e8c98d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245120} {:answer/id #uuid "840a4e80-3153-50a1-8616-d916253b88ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245477} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc7422d-5fbd-501a-8a53-a1ed60d54f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246170} {:answer/id #uuid "db735a97-9407-5ac8-9622-03d7787e8b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246261}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "840a4e80-3153-50a1-8616-d916253b88ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245477} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd688132-28be-5fd2-82e8-52d094605862" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90603bab-bd0f-5ff5-b578-328ff040206a" :question/math-academy-id "q-110859" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{(1+x+y)^{3}},\\, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0\\}.$\n\n![](images/99/996bd092be07e2596e30739cc4249e8ed8ef859540067a94e0f9cdd05f9e1f84.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }[{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{[-\\frac{c}{2(1+x+y)^{2}}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\infty }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{2(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{c}{2(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-\\frac{c}{2(1+x)}]}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{2}=1\\,⇒\\,c=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a66952d-4e4b-5249-8f44-27d6fd198ea8" :knowledge-point/title "Joint PDFs for Continuous Random Variables: Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69feed6a-dbe0-5506-a4e6-6b2624ebbe66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245251} {:answer/id #uuid "7db45604-045f-586d-a84a-2cd74a531cf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ and $g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245420} {:answer/id #uuid "90ddcbc6-45ee-5f67-a4a4-2c97b5e22a9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ and $h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245578} {:answer/id #uuid "ae8981f3-eb30-5f81-a243-b408ef0638bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245869} {:answer/id #uuid "b25cc9ae-6119-5fd1-9fcf-65a673d6191e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g$ and $h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7db45604-045f-586d-a84a-2cd74a531cf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ and $g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245420} :answer-field/id #uuid "920732ef-0a8f-5b60-a8af-94d2efc833cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246800}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29c9baf2-abe1-5681-94f0-fc4fef002362" :question/math-academy-id "q-110862" :question/problem "Let $D=[0,2]\\times [1,2].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\nI. $f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{3},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $g(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $h(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-y}{6},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{xy}{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{2y}{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $g(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}g(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}g(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x}{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}1\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[y]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the first condition. For example,\n\n$$\nh(0,2)=\\frac{0-2}{6}=-\\frac{1}{3}≱0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$f$ and $g$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a66952d-4e4b-5249-8f44-27d6fd198ea8" :knowledge-point/title "Joint PDFs for Continuous Random Variables: Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "596fb15b-9278-5dfe-92e9-2a47c3fb8ee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476396} {:answer/id #uuid "66e5a963-fe67-5131-9387-46c125d4871b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476435} {:answer/id #uuid "8199d30b-51f8-5be5-a28a-a65da3d6c2d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476487} {:answer/id #uuid "97fc35cf-14d0-5200-a801-d88272cd7dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f$ and $h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476536} {:answer/id #uuid "a03baadf-376f-5b6b-b06b-019bdd1343dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "596fb15b-9278-5dfe-92e9-2a47c3fb8ee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$h$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476396} :answer-field/id #uuid "290d3510-e616-5c9a-9571-b43c639908e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6513aec-b650-5caf-b4d5-5b3d2bca02b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-110866" :question/problem "Let $D=[1,e]\\times [1,e].$ Which of the following functions are valid joint probability density functions?\n\nI. $f(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4xy,\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $g(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x-y}{4e},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $h(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{xy},\\, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$  is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the second condition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{1}^{e}4xy\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}[{\\int }_{1}^{e}4xy\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{[2{x}^{2}y]}_{x=1}^{x=e}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}2({e}^{2}-1)y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[({e}^{2}-1){y}^{2}]_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =({e}^{2}-1)^{2} \\\\  & \\ne 1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is *not* a valid probability density function because it does not satisfy the first condition. For example,\n\n$$\ng(1,e)=\\frac{2-e}{4e}≱0.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is a valid probability density function. Indeed, we have $h(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$  and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{R}^{2}}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}h(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}[{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{xy}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{[\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{y}]}_{x=1}^{x=e}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$h$ only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162069 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "129940fb-a409-55bc-9852-f68f4f9ffd11" :knowledge-point/title "Joint PDFs for Continuous Random Variables: Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16130171-28c9-5e26-847c-56cba78bf71a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476239} {:answer/id #uuid "804ae345-3ee0-562b-a4f2-fd882a705b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476486} {:answer/id #uuid "962381dc-a808-5d2b-a4df-151f0ed19e65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476529} {:answer/id #uuid "9941cb5d-24d9-55fe-afb0-b8b524fa2de5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476537} {:answer/id #uuid "ebecbae9-4e6e-51c2-aa5e-8c7b1ff2b9a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16130171-28c9-5e26-847c-56cba78bf71a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476239} :answer-field/id #uuid "eed8ad9e-e0da-5240-bd75-5bd17b2051da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbbae5a7-4143-5b60-9c21-0b9858325bf7" :question/math-academy-id "q-110867" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c,\\, & 0\\le y<1-x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y<1-x\\le 1\\}.$\n\n![](images/ab/ab6a4fce220150c53a70ca236843203460a7040cc1a1b5dfe664c6d45dd93ac1.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}c\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1-x}c\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[cy]}_{y=0}^{y=1-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}c(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-\\frac{c(1-x)^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{2}=1\\,⇒\\,c=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162069 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "129940fb-a409-55bc-9852-f68f4f9ffd11" :knowledge-point/title "Joint PDFs for Continuous Random Variables: Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1002abf0-16a9-545d-9373-728ba3f94fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e+{e}^{-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244461} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4e204d-0b4c-54ba-87b4-56d60185143a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e-{e}^{-1}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245138} {:answer/id #uuid "6801d7b6-a3db-5055-923c-7f3de5384e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e-{e}^{-1}-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245235} {:answer/id #uuid "b2bebd39-305e-5fff-9a73-443b09049842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e+{e}^{-1}-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245903} {:answer/id #uuid "cb61f56d-fad9-5c38-98e3-ebb91e331c3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e+{e}^{-1}+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2bebd39-305e-5fff-9a73-443b09049842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e+{e}^{-1}-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245903} :answer-field/id #uuid "8671c1fd-5e35-5984-8241-cdbb795f5459" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5fb35e0-0098-50b2-92bd-fb03dcef42bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-110977" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c{e}^{x-y},\\, & 0\\le x<2y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x<2y\\le 2\\}.$\n\n![](images/60/60acb4126a456e51e00db8bbeb2f8dbc6a773a4792dda7838d2aa6a3553fa739.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}c{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}c{e}^{x-y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating  $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}c{e}^{x-y}\\,dx\\,dy & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-y}[{\\int }_{0}^{2y}{e}^{x}\\,dx]dy \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-y}[{e}^{x}]_{x=0}^{x=2y}\\,dy \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-y}({e}^{2y}-{e}^{0})dy \\\\  & =c{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{y}-{e}^{-y})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c{\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{y}-{e}^{-y})\\,dy & =c[{e}^{y}+{e}^{-y}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =c({e}^{1}+{e}^{-1})-({e}^{0}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =c(e+{e}^{-1}-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\nc(e+{e}^{-1}-2)=1\\,⇒\\,c=\\frac{1}{e+{e}^{-1}-2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162069 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "129940fb-a409-55bc-9852-f68f4f9ffd11" :knowledge-point/title "Joint PDFs for Continuous Random Variables: Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7262c3f0-8d17-5510-940c-dfd096f6e29b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245334} {:answer/id #uuid "a052dbb7-5c7d-5743-ab74-b24d3428a419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245739} {:answer/id #uuid "b86aa479-c5c2-5a5d-a05d-a655be0a0795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245963} {:answer/id #uuid "c4391b2b-a3c5-5ab3-93fa-4120a6ab86ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246058} {:answer/id #uuid "f0571465-3cc4-52ab-8fdc-d935dbfeecab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7262c3f0-8d17-5510-940c-dfd096f6e29b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245334} :answer-field/id #uuid "70f04b94-d5fc-5445-a232-b2fc977bdc31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf819c75-7419-5272-b743-a86ae3746c07" :question/math-academy-id "q-110985" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c(1-xy),\\, & {y}^{2}\\le x\\le y, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le y\\}.$\n\n![](images/1e/1ef0c7b289e24a98366c64b9a213c4f67d2fc63157b3319f0118eb16c514097e.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y}c(1-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating  $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y}c(1-xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[cx-\\frac{cy{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x={y}^{2}}^{x=y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-({y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2}))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}c(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2})\\,dy & =c{[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{8}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{6}}{12}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}-\\frac{c}{8}-\\frac{c}{3}+\\frac{c}{12}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{8}=1\\,⇒\\,c=8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247194 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162069 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "129940fb-a409-55bc-9852-f68f4f9ffd11" :knowledge-point/title "Joint PDFs for Continuous Random Variables: Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "083c51fc-aa18-56dc-b3c0-124a775f8946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244378} {:answer/id #uuid "675427f6-ac1f-521a-b7bc-59f1e3b0b6a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245221} {:answer/id #uuid "714d2134-55a2-57cb-8d39-8bf99301077a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245324} {:answer/id #uuid "98716022-d6bf-54d3-874d-c54fc12b52e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245665} {:answer/id #uuid "ba1eb9d4-e845-50fa-86d1-d1a588f1aaf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "083c51fc-aa18-56dc-b3c0-124a775f8946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244378} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae9c6475-f39e-5cd6-88f8-b618ffddc3b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "800aa4d6-ad4a-5a10-9d04-aca005ce255a" :question/math-academy-id "q-110986" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y},\\, & 1\\le x\\le e,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{x}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{x}\\}.$\n\n![](images/26/268e469b0306d22e6b6dc05905a9d56a1cabd4b0e405f741ff458ca241440d4a.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{c}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx=1.\n$$\n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{c}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{c}{yx\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c}{x}[{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{x}}\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\\,dy]\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c}{x}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)]_{y=e}^{y={e}^{x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{x})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e))\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{c\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}dx & ={[\\frac{c{ln}^{2}⁡x}{2}]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{c{ln}^{2}⁡e}{2}-\\frac{c{ln}^{2}⁡1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{2}=1\\,⇒\\,c=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476879 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162069 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "129940fb-a409-55bc-9852-f68f4f9ffd11" :knowledge-point/title "Joint PDFs for Continuous Random Variables: Non-Rectangular Domains"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "333af451-7d02-57c5-ba08-cb8404414570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476317} {:answer/id #uuid "78dc14a6-5564-5474-8294-3041295ae670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476473} {:answer/id #uuid "8655f0e3-9e9f-5a17-99ac-09a449fe24c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476495} {:answer/id #uuid "e14679e8-c25f-5198-a594-67ee2a871294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476704} {:answer/id #uuid "e643ecef-744b-599b-893e-0a9a3566682b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "333af451-7d02-57c5-ba08-cb8404414570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476317} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1777218-b93b-5f9b-9c6b-9c80483a6a16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18816b37-fa3d-5d53-82e0-4019189f108e" :question/math-academy-id "q-110989" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}c\\sqrt{x-y},\\, & 0\\le x-{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$" :question/worked-solution "To verify that $f(x,y)$ is a valid joint probability density function, we must check the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$\n\n- ${∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=1$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x-{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x\\le 1\\}.$\n\n![](images/c7/c7e555ed139343a1a83e0b05bdb9a13be502271b76e9b9bbdf7ed5f3e869fcd0.png)\n\nNow, let's check our conditions:\n\n- Since $c>0,$ we clearly have $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ So the first condition is satisfied.\n\n- For the second condition to be satisfied, we must have \n\n$$\n{∬}_{{R}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-{x}^{2}}^{x}c\\sqrt{x-y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating  $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-{x}^{2}}^{x}c\\sqrt{x-y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c[{\\int }_{x-{x}^{2}}^{x}c\\sqrt{x-y}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c{[-\\frac{2}{3}(x-y)^{3/2}]}_{y=x-{x}^{2}}^{y=x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}c(-0+\\frac{2}{3}(x-x+{x}^{2})^{3/2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2c{x}^{3}}{3}dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2c{x}^{3}}{3}dx & ={[\\frac{c{x}^{4}}{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{c}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{6}=1\\,⇒\\,c=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162070 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2bcf1eb2-cf04-5dd0-bb43-682c7e9cd953" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Using a Joint PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29acc45f-d9a2-5198-b52c-9661da5934be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244682} {:answer/id #uuid "95f053d5-8795-52c1-b722-00db9f29acbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245630} {:answer/id #uuid "b23c800d-72d5-5790-993e-23742300ba63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245898} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e30e05-845a-5458-9e2a-b3871b7cd5e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246081} {:answer/id #uuid "f80a92e0-f18c-5c27-833b-a6c8d19d4085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246493}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b23c800d-72d5-5790-993e-23742300ba63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245898} :answer-field/id #uuid "27ad5964-4ae5-57fc-9939-768924881d76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8656cfbb-6643-57f2-9ab0-17cded63f444" :question/math-academy-id "q-111163" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{(1+x+y)^{3}},\\, & 0\\le y\\le x, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X+Y<2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=\\{0\\le y\\le x\\},$ and the event $A=\\{X+Y<2\\}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/f7/f7a0f0fb9d20a4b88d0aa4c395e2adb1d62195601ce756f57a48becc6ab38681.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y<2) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}\\frac{4}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}\\frac{4}{(1+x+y)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{2}{(1+x+y)^{2}}]}_{x=y}^{x=2-y}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{2}{(1+2y)^{2}}-\\frac{2}{9})\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{1+2y}-\\frac{2y}{9}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{2}{9}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162070 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2bcf1eb2-cf04-5dd0-bb43-682c7e9cd953" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Using a Joint PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cf547f1-cafb-5651-b52e-9ea1096ccc4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476410} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbaee51-853a-5eae-ab51-f2dc1b8aa74f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476508} {:answer/id #uuid "bca77e37-e9f3-5733-bc3e-94c730f8c1e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476631} {:answer/id #uuid "bd05a066-1270-563d-af7e-42b8e2066990" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476632} {:answer/id #uuid "d5734f49-20f7-55a9-a2d2-84ce184340ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8fbaee51-853a-5eae-ab51-f2dc1b8aa74f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476508} :answer-field/id #uuid "58ddfa6b-9730-58d4-a05c-c147cc41e022" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186476792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6736cbc0-043f-5dbb-9f00-d4843e825805" :question/math-academy-id "q-111166" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P((X-1)^{2}+{Y}^{2}<1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,2]\\times [0,2],$ and the event $A=\\{(X-1)^{2}+{Y}^{2}<1\\}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below, which is a semicircle of radius $R=1:$\n\n![](images/f7/f7f9f016d5f121b2153c1833c959be76f5a11eabb121533dd11839d6da973814.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P((X-1)^{2}+{Y}^{2}<1) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{∬}_{A}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\text{Area}(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\pi {R}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162070 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2bcf1eb2-cf04-5dd0-bb43-682c7e9cd953" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Using a Joint PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f927547-7aa7-5076-8d36-ac157d2b457c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244741} {:answer/id #uuid "5780513f-168d-5408-a894-514183892c66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245085} {:answer/id #uuid "96f1be30-a397-5e4f-a5fc-6ac756ff7738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245645} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c65bdf-5bd7-5455-8b12-4605a010ab42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245904} {:answer/id #uuid "bc008801-a322-5a70-a5f4-032dd023a42b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc008801-a322-5a70-a5f4-032dd023a42b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245989} :answer-field/id #uuid "f50bb897-e2b5-507e-974e-a5e6f1881a90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5199c42f-aff1-55e5-b62b-a83ffcaa9370" :question/math-academy-id "q-111167" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X+Y>1).$" :question/worked-solution "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,1]\\times [0,1],$ and the event $A=\\{X+Y>1\\}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/58/58c1beb1b13680e330260d93fdf8e60460e2bfeae4c1cbbc2fce8b261afe7ea1.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>1) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{A}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(A) \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162070 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2bcf1eb2-cf04-5dd0-bb43-682c7e9cd953" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Using a Joint PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58338181-f5f2-53c4-ba2c-1eea5f78b0ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371571} {:answer/id #uuid "51c241d2-b827-5429-8bbc-12cb2e50febb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371572} {:answer/id #uuid "08d78c40-67ca-5552-bfbc-85ea24759319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371573} {:answer/id #uuid "6cfd8f25-4f0e-55fd-8077-79f56797ab4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371574} {:answer/id #uuid "94df0702-4a0f-564a-b2f1-d0fd90b62b07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58338181-f5f2-53c4-ba2c-1eea5f78b0ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371571} :answer-field/id #uuid "63d8ef8e-e282-5085-bd0c-37645c87d08b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "821858ee-46eb-57a4-9f6b-cec1ae6e7ca5" :question/math-academy-id "q-111251" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(Y<X<Y+2).$" :question/worked-solution "If $f(x,y)$ is a joint probability density function for the random variables $X$ and $Y$ and $A⊂{R}^{2},$ then\n\n$$\nP((X,Y)\\in A)={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonnegative in the region $D=[0,3]\\times [0,1],$ and the event $A=\\{Y<X<Y+2\\}$ corresponds to a subset of $D,$ shown as the shaded region below:\n\n![](images/f4/f4a0cc357eee835c84ef08524d27e3c30b98d58e1b6b5083b7df6a66ce987b6b.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y<X<Y+2) & ={∬}_{A}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{A}\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\text{Area}(A) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
